/**
 * Colorado Real Estate Law and Practice (48 classroom hours) — module question bank.
 *
 * THE FIRST OF SIX COURSES TOTALLING 168 HOURS. C.R.S. § 12-10-203(4)(a) requires 48 hours in real estate
 * law and real estate practice, 48 in understanding and preparation of Colorado real estate contracts, and
 * "A TOTAL OF SEVENTY-TWO HOURS" from four named subjects — allocating none among the four. Rule 2.1.A.2
 * makes that allocation: 48 exact, 48 exact, and then "NOT LESS THAN" 24, 8, 8 and 32.
 *
 * WRITTEN TO THE COMMISSION'S OWN OUTLINE. Unlike Arizona and Washington, where this project recorded as its
 * first submission gate that the regulator's prescribed course content could not be obtained, THE COLORADO
 * COMMISSION PUBLISHES ALL SIX OUTLINES. This course is written to the Real Estate Law and Practice outline
 * approved 1 April 1998, whose five topics carry the Commission's own hours: 10, 7, 12, 12, 7 = 48. The five
 * modules are those five topics.
 *
 * THIS IS THE NATIONAL COURSE OF THE SIX. The Commission's stated objective: "a common body of knowledge ...
 * as well as RELATED FEDERAL LAWS PRACTICED IN A MAJORITY OF STATES ON A NATIONAL LEVEL." Colorado's own
 * brokerage relationship law is the Contracts and Regulations course. NO ITEM HERE MAY PRESENT A GENERAL
 * RULE AS COLORADO'S, and several items exist to catch a student who would carry one into a transaction.
 *
 * THE THREE COLORADO PROVISIONS THAT DISPLACE THE GENERAL ACCOUNT, flagged in every module that touches
 * them:
 *   § 12-10-403(2) — "A broker shall be considered a TRANSACTION-BROKER unless a single agency relationship
 *                    is established through a WRITTEN AGREEMENT." Agency is opt-in, in writing. Compare
 *                    Washington, where buyer agency arises BY DEFAULT from performing services.
 *   § 12-10-406   — "A broker shall not establish dual agency with any seller, landlord, buyer, or tenant."
 *                    The whole section. No consent mechanism, no exception. Washington permits limited dual
 *                    agency with separately initialed consent; Arizona permits it with prior written consent
 *                    of all parties.
 *   § 12-10-403(6)(b) — the relationship with a designated broker "SHALL NOT EXTEND to the employing broker
 *                    ... and shall not extend to the firm" — while § 12-10-408(2)(e)(II) preserves
 *                    supervision and vicarious liability. TWO DIFFERENT THINGS; no item may merge them.
 *
 * THE ONE PRESCRIBED WORDING IN THIS COURSE. Rule 6.12 requires a broker preparing a CMA or BPO for any
 * reason other than an anticipated sale or purchase to include a notice stating, in the rule's own words:
 * "This evaluation was prepared by a licensed real estate broker and is not an appraisal. This evaluation
 * cannot be used for the purposes of obtaining financing." The same rule PROHIBITS completing CMAs or BPOs
 * used to obtain financing.
 *
 * Each module carries one applied item, five engagement items and five remediation items. The correct
 * answer is authored at index 0 and permuted deterministically per item id at delivery. The public delivery
 * path strips correctChoice and rationale, and a test proves it. The 80 percent engagement threshold and
 * 100 percent applied threshold are STATECOURSE STANDARDS; the Commission's outline requires that "testing
 * is required to establish individual's satisfactory completion of the statutory requirements," and these
 * items are NOT that test.
 *
 * WHAT WAS NOT READ, AND WHICH THEREFORE APPEARS IN NO ITEM: every Commission-approved form; every
 * Commission position statement; C.R.S. §§ 12-10-217, 12-10-219, 12-10-222, 12-10-218, 12-10-304 and
 * 12-10-602; § 38-35.5-101; § 38-12-101 et seq.; § 38-35-125; the Colorado Common Interest Ownership Act;
 * Colorado's statute of frauds, water law, recording, mechanics' lien, adverse possession, foreclosure and
 * public trustee, condemnation, escheat, entity, trust, probate, zoning, special district and property tax
 * statutes; the Colorado Fair Housing Act; and RESPA, TILA, TRID, ECOA, the federal Fair Housing Act, the
 * ADA, antitrust, ILSA, the TCPA, the UCC and the federal lead-based paint regulations. Every figure in
 * every computational item is supplied inside the item as a given.
 *
 * Course version 1.0 submission draft — NOT APPROVED. No Colorado qualifying education hours are available.
 */

